## Details
- K: 222x222 float64, dense, mixed signs, no Toeplitz/banded/symmetric
  structure (mean |K_ij| ~ 0.0068 for all offsets |i-j| = 1..11).
- IMPORTANT numerical note: numpy's default SVD driver (dgesdd) returns an inconsistent
  factorization for this matrix (||U^T K V - diag(s)|| ~ 2.8 despite U, V orthogonal).
  All analysis uses scipy's 'gesvd' driver, verified consistent to 9.1e-15.
- Singular values decay geometrically, sigma_i ~ 0.857^(i-1) (log10 slope -0.067 per index),
  from 1 down to 1.74e-15; condition number ~ 5.740e+14: severely ill-posed.
- y: length 222, ||y|| = 1.9796, std 0.1324.
- Spectral coefficients b_i = (U^T y)_i follow sigma_i down to i ~ 25-40, then plateau at
  ~0.024-0.029 (noise floor). Picard ratios |b_i|/sigma_i are O(1) up to i ~ 40 (all < 10 for
  i <= 40) and then grow without bound (up to ~1e13 at i = 221). Hence the discrete Picard
  condition does NOT hold on the full spectrum; it holds only on the effective-rank subspace
  that regularization must retain.
- Noise estimate: sigma_hat = rms(b_i, i >= 60) = 0.0291
  (1.4826*MAD gives 0.0262); relative noise sigma_hat/||y|| = 0.0147.
  Tail statistics: skew -0.41, excess kurtosis 0.38, KS p = 0.31 vs
  fitted Normal, runs test z = -1.1, y lag-1..5 autocorrelations all |r| <= 0.09.
  -> The noise appears white, homoscedastic and near-Gaussian. The whitening code path
     (make_whitener) exists in solution.py but is NOT activated; if the noise were colored,
     one would transform K' = L^-1 K, y' = L^-1 y (L = Cholesky factor of the noise
     covariance) and apply all discrepancy/GCV criteria to whitened residuals.
- effective_rank = 26: smallest TSVD truncation t with ||r_t|| <= 1.0*sigma_hat*sqrt(n-t);
  equivalently where the singular-value curve sigma_i crosses the noise level (from i ~ 24
  onward sigma_i < sigma_hat).
